				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$ \begin{array}{@{}rcl@{}} \text{NSP}_f &=& \frac{ \text{FN} }{ N }. \end{array} $$\end{document}NSPf=FNN.Then, PR, computed as
4\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$ P_R = 1 - \frac{ \text{NSP}_f }{ \text{NSP}_i }{{=1-\frac{ \text{FN} }{ \text{FN}+\text{TP} }=\frac{ \text{TP} }{ \text{FN}+\text{TP} }}}, $$\end{document}PR=1−NSPfNSPi=1−FNFN+TP=TPFN+TP,measures the effectiveness of the prediction. To assess the trade-off between cost and effectiveness, we defined metrics:
5\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
